* Frame-level sharing at PAGE granularity, inside 1 MB segments.
*
* WHAT THIS SETTLES.  docs/05-CP67-PRIOR-ART.md carries this repository's
* most load-bearing conclusion: that moving from 64 KB to 1 MB segments does
* NOT cost a 16x loss of sharing granularity, because sharing need not be
* done by repointing a segment table entry.  CP-67 ran 4 KB pages in 1 MB
* segments and shared at 4 KB, by giving each machine its own page table and
* sharing the FRAMES.
*
* That rested on IBM documentation from 1973.  This executes it.
*
*   docs/04-SHARED-SEGMENTS.md  what VM/370's segment-level sharing costs at
*                               1 MB: CMSOLD forces the first megabyte common
*   docs/05-CP67-PRIOR-ART.md   the argument that the cost is avoidable
*   THIS TEST                   the avoidance, on the real hardware
*
* THE SHAPE.  Two address spaces with their own segment AND page tables, and
* identical virtual addresses in both:
*
*   virtual 01005000  seg 16 page 5   A -> 01100000   B -> 01100000  SHARED
*   virtual 01006000  seg 16 page 6   A -> 01200000   B -> 01300000  private
*
* Write under A, read under B at 01005000 -- must match, because one frame.
* Write under both at 01006000 -- must differ, though it is the same virtual
* address in the same 1 MB segment.
*
* Both holding at once means 4 KB sharing granularity and 4 KB isolation
* granularity COEXIST INSIDE ONE 1 MB SEGMENT -- precisely what VM/370's
* SYSHRSG cannot express and what CP-67's FIRSTSP/LASTSP could.
*
* All three frames are ABOVE THE LINE (17, 18, 19 MB), so this exercises the
* case that matters rather than a convenient low-storage one.
*
* THE STEP THAT WILL BITE ANYONE REPEATING THIS: PTLB.  Changing CR1 does not
* invalidate the translation lookaside buffer.  Without a PTLB after each
* switch, the second address space reads through the first one's cached
* translations, and the test either passes for the wrong reason or fails
* confusingly.  CP has only 4 PTLB sites today and will need more.
*
* NEGATIVE CONTROLS -- BOTH RUN, BOTH BEHAVE
* ------------------------------------------
* This passed first time, which after the LRA test earned some suspicion.  So
* it was broken deliberately, twice, to prove it discriminates:
*
*   r 40D4=01400000   B's page 5 -> a different frame
*                     -> 001011, the sharing proof fails
*   r 40D8=01200000   B's page 6 -> A's frame
*                     -> 001012, the isolation proof fails
*
* Append either line after the pokes below to reproduce.  A test that cannot
* be made to fail has not been shown to test anything.
*
* HOW TO READ THE RESULT
*   00600A   pass.  Page-granular sharing AND isolation in one 1 MB segment.
*   001011   THE SHARING PROOF FAILED -- the frame is not actually shared.
*   001012   THE ISOLATION PROOF FAILED -- the private page is shared.
*   001013   isolation failed in the other direction (A saw B's byte).
*   001001   real storage above the line unusable at all -- a more basic
*            fault than anything to do with translation.
*   001021   shared frame wrong when read at its real address, DAT off.
*   001022   A's private frame wrong.   001023  B's private frame wrong.
*   000DED   program check -- read the old PSW at X'150'.
*
* WRITE PROTECTION IS A SEPARATE TEST.  CP-67's mechanism has two halves:
* per-page frame sharing for granularity, and storage keys -- shared pages at
* key 0, users running PSW key F -- for read-only enforcement.  Those are
* independent mechanisms and one change at a time has worked all day.
*
* LAYOUT
*   A: SEGTAB 3000  PAGETAB0 3080  PAGE16 30C0  CR1 00003001
*   B: SEGTAB 4000  PAGETAB0 4080  PAGE16 40C0  CR1 00004001
*   Segment 0 is identity-mapped for pages 0-15 in both, so the code at 2000
*   and the tables themselves stay addressable with DAT on.
*   STL 1 gives 32 segment entries, so entry 16 exists.
*
* Built by build/share1.py.
*
